---
id: org.dxos.agent-delegation
name: Agents, Sessions and Delegation
version: 0.1.0
---

How agent work is identified, split and handed off. Three tiers name the units:

- **Agent** — the unit of identity, permissions and access control. An agent is invited to spaces.
  It has one main durable object: the entry point for external events, with no inference of its
  own, that delegates to worker sessions. It has a home space that holds its memory (RDF store),
  its ECHO database, its access tokens and its skills.
- **Session** — the unit of work. Each session is its own durable object with its own feed and
  task list. A session attached to an agent inherits that agent's permissions. A standalone
  session (no agent) is still supported.
- **Thread** — a microtask inside a session; what Claude calls a subagent.

Delegation has two scopes. **Intra-agent** delegation hands work to sessions and threads of one
agent. **Cross-agent** delegation hands work to another agent: a separate identity, separate
spaces and a separate entry point.

The task list in the project or session is the control plane. Ephemeral messages (wake, start,
stop) sit on top of it and carry no state of their own.

